{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and leader of the County of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *Early Life* Raymond III was born around 1140 and was still a minor when his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna acted as regent until he came of age. Raymond III was taken to the court of King Baldwin III of Jerusalem where he was raised and educated. \n\nAfter coming of age Raymond III took control of the County of Tripoli. He was a skilled military leader and expanded his territories through strategic marriages and alliances. *Reign* Raymond III was a key figure in the politics of the Crusader states and played a significant role in the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a close relative of the ruling family of Jerusalem and was often involved in the governance of the kingdom. In 1174 Raymond III was appointed as regent for King Baldwin IV who was suffering from leprosy. \n\nDuring his regency Raymond III worked to strengthen the military defenses of the kingdom and to secure alliances with neighboring states. However his efforts were often thwarted by the internal conflicts and power struggles within the kingdom. *Later Life and Legacy* Raymond III was a key player in the events leading up to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He was one of the few leaders who survived the battle and was taken prisoner by Saladin. He was later released and died in 1187 without an heir. The County of Tripoli was inherited by his godson Raymond IV who was the son of Bohemond III of Antioch. Raymond III's legacy is complex and multifaceted. He was a skilled leader and a shrewd politician who played a significant role in the history of the Crusader states. Despite his many accomplishments his failure to produce an heir and his role in the events leading up to the Battle of Hattin 